It's not a question for OpenTelemetry. It's a comment about the API. Here's my MongoDB driver instrumentation when using `ActivitySource`:

```csharp
var activity = ActivitySource.StartActivity(ActivityName, ActivityKind.Client);

if (activity == null)
{
    return;
}

activity.DisplayName = $"mongodb.{@event.CommandName}";

if (activity.IsAllDataRequested)
{
    activity.AddTag("db.type", "mongo");
    activity.AddTag("db.instance", @event.DatabaseNamespace.DatabaseName);
    // many more tags
```

I add all the tags *after* the activity is started. Which means they won't be available to an `ActivityListener`, and downstream tracing clients.

Previously, the "start" event via `DiagnosticListener` would carry a payload of context that I would then use to enrich the `Activity` further before it got recorded by a telemetry client (OpenTelemetry in my case).

```csharp
private void Handle(CommandStartedEvent @event)
{
    var activity = new Activity(ActivityName);

    _diagnosticListener.OnActivityExport(activity, @event);

    if (_diagnosticListener.IsEnabled(ActivityStartEventName, @event))
    {
        _diagnosticListener.StartActivity(activity, @event);
    }
    else
    {
        activity.Start();
    }
```

Then, my diagnostic subscriber, I would add the tags if all data was requested, then bridge in this case to OTel (though that's immaterial):

```csharp
public override void OnStartActivity(Activity activity, object payload)
{
    if (!(payload is CommandStartedEvent message))
    {
        return;
    }

    activity.DisplayName = $"mongodb.{message.CommandName}";

    if (activity.IsAllDataRequested)
    {
        activity.AddTag("db.type", "mongo");
        activity.AddTag("db.instance", message.DatabaseNamespace.DatabaseName);
        // many more tags
    }
    _activitySource.Start(activity, ActivityKind.Client);
```

I don't record the activity to a telemetry client until after I've added all the tags based on the context being passed through. This is what I meant by "tags are missing". The events are recorded in the `ActivitySource` method, but because I only add all the tags if `IsAllDataRequested`, I've lost the opportunity to have these tags added or context available in the "Start" event.

Hi @jbogard, sorry this issue has been quiet a bit. @tarekgh and I were taking a look at issues like this one that came in late in .NET 5 to see what we could do in .NET 6. I could see a few potential paths forward here:
1. We change the past guidance to indicate that tags/baggage/other Activity content is not fully populated in the Start event. Instrumentors would add it after the Activity is started and Listeners would pick it up during the Stop event. For people who were using DiagnosticListener in the past to transmit Activities that are now using ActivitySource this is certainly a change in behavior but perhaps not an impactful one?
2. .NET could create a new API such as ActivitySource.CreateUnstartedActivity(...) that has the same parameters as StartActivity(). Then you could write code that looked like this which retains behavior closer to what you had before:

````C#
// this invokes ActivityListener.Sample callback but not ActivityListener.Start
var activity = ActivitySource.CreateUnstartedActivity(ActivityName, ActivityKind.Client);

if (activity == null)
{
    return;
}

activity.DisplayName = $"mongodb.{@event.CommandName}";

if (activity.IsAllDataRequested)
{
    activity.AddTag("db.type", "mongo");
    activity.AddTag("db.instance", @event.DatabaseNamespace.DatabaseName);
    // many more tags
}

// now the ActivityListener.StartActivity callback gets invoked and the full set of Tags, Baggage, etc 
// that you want to provide can be present
activity.Start();
````
3. Some other alternative that you have in mind?

I think the discussion before the holidays was converging towards option (2), but I wanted to confirm. If so we can firm up the details and get the ball rolling on this API. Thanks!

